.0268096923828125, 0.04193115234375, -0.0081939697265625, -0.01372528076171875, 0.058746337890625, 0.0013113021850585938, 0.04290771484375, -0.03411865234375, -0.005462646484375, 0.00041174888610839844, 0.00452423095703125, 0.0041046142578125, -0.03399658203125, -0.00341796875, -0.04803466796875, 0.0009083747863769531, -0.004909515380859375, -0.005146026611328125, -0.001018524169921875, -0.0244140625, -0.0020904541015625, -0.0168609619140625, -0.005657196044921875, -0.01015472412109375, 0.0280609130859375, 0.00711822509765625, 0.01380157470703125, -0.004669189453125, 0.005039215087890625, -0.0115203857421875, 0.00963592529296875, -0.00933074951171875, -0.01213836669921875, 0.018798828125, 0.061279296875, 0.01611328125, 0.0236358642578125, -0.00728607177734375, -0.022796630859375, 0.0189361572265625, 0.006778717041015625, 0.0044097900390625, -0.02008056640625, 0.023529052734375, -0.01702880859375, -0.038909912109375, 0.01708984375, -0.0057525634765625, 0.0031375885009765625, 0.01476287841796875, 0.01557159423828125, 0.007152557373046875, -0.01053619384765625, 0.00278472900390625, -0.00429534912109375, -0.0193939208984375, -0.0053558349609375, 0.0193939208984375, -0.0023345947265625, -0.026092529296875, -0.01026153564453125, 0.02392578125, 0.0032634735107421875, 0.0228118896484375, 0.0258331298828125, -0.0102386474609375, 0.00514984130859375, -0.0013895034790039062, -0.0239105224609375, 0.0048065185546875, 0.01549530029296875, -0.017730712890625, -0.0256805419921875, 0.0108642578125, 0.031646728515625, 0.0027408599853515625, -0.00452423095703125, -0.0012979507446289062, 0.001922607421875, -0.01904296875, 0.036376953125, -0.002689361572265625, 0.0213623046875, 0.0738525390625, 0.0185699462890625, -0.0270843505859375, -0.0124053955078125, 0.048187255859375, -0.01477813720703125, 0.01541900634765625, 0.013458251953125, 0.0389404296875, 0.00444793701171875, 0.012939453125, -0.002166748046875, 0.04364013671875, 0.01580810546875, 0.04241943359375, -0.007259368896484375, -0.0096282958984375, -0.0110015869140625, 0.029510498046875, 0.01056671142578125, 0.00337982177734375, 0.050750732421875, -0.025360107421875, 0.005664825439453125, 0.00818634033203125, 0.01299285888671875, 0.01155853271484375, 0.0024242401123046875, 0.033660888671875, -0.0049591064453125, 0.05010986328125, -0.00957489013671875, 0.019775390625, -0.0167388916015625, 0.010467529296875, -0.0032863616943359375, -0.0198211669921875, 0.01204681396484375, -0.0026397705078125, -0.0169830322265625, 0.0210723876953125, 0.035736083984375, -0.015899658203125, -0.0138397216796875, -0.01250457763671875, 0.00556182861328125, -0.0161895751953125, 0.00040531158447265625, 0.047607421875, 0.0300140380859375, 0.0098419189453125, -0.01163482666015625, -0.01800537109375, -0.005535125732421875, -0.0033283233642578125, -0.0181732177734375, 0.005649566650390625, -0.039093017578125, -0.00811767578125, -0.0188446044921875, -0.01038360595703125, 0.022308349609375, 0.0111541748046875, -0.013763427734375, -0.023590087890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\n# Introduction\n\nLarge-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines typically optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, without considering the relationships between different generative tasks. A central challenge is not only how to curate each task-specific corpus, but also how to organize heterogeneous supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n## Capabilities vs. Tasks\n\nA **capability** is a distinct, transferable generative skill that a model can acquire and apply across multiple tasks. In contrast, a **task** is a specific instance or application that exercises one or more capabilities. For example, \"text-to-image (T2I) synthesis\" is a capability, while \"generate an image of a golden retriever playing in a park\" is a task that exercises this capability. Similarly, \"image editing\" is a capability, while \"add a red sofa to a living room image\" is a task requiring both T2I and image editing capabilities.\n\n| Aspect           | Capability (e.g., T2I, Editing) | Task (e.g., Generate, Edit)         |\n|------------------|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Definition       | Generalizable generative skill   | Specific instance or application    |\n| Scope            | Broad, reusable across tasks     | Narrow, context-dependent           |\n| Example          | Image editing                    | Remove a tree from a photo          |\n| Dependencies     | May require other capabilities   | Requires certain capabilities       |\n\nThese dependencies among capabilities are critical for designing effective training curricula. For example, the ability to perform image editing presupposes that the model can already generate coherent images from text.\n\n**Novelty and Contributions:**  \nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure that systematically organizes data collection and training according to the progression of model capabilities. Unlike prior work\u2014such as DALL-E 2 [1], Imagen [2], and Parti [3]\u2014which often treat each generative task and its dataset independently, our framework explicitly models and operationalizes the dependencies among generative skills (capabilities), enabling curriculum learning that reflects these relationships. Our key contributions are:\n- A modular data infrastructure that aligns data curation and curriculum learning with the dependency structure of generative capabilities.\n- Three interoperable data engines for text-image grounding, inter-image transformation, and image-knowledge association, each tailored to a specific capability.\n- A curriculum learning protocol that dynamically schedules data and tasks based on capability dependencies and ongoing evaluation, with explicit transition criteria.\n- Capability-aware evaluation and gap-aware resampling to iteratively improve data coverage and model performance.\n- Large-scale empirical validation, demonstrating improved performance over baselines on compositional and photorealistic image generation benchmarks.\n\n# Methods\n\nWe present a capability-driven framework for organizing data and training curricula in large-scale image generation. The following sections detail the formalization of capabilities and tasks, the construction of the capability dependency graph, the design and interoperability of data engines, the role of caption experts and expert construction, the curriculum learning protocol, evaluation and resampling strategies, and quality assurance procedures.\n\n## Formalization of Capabilities and Tasks\n\nAs defined above, a **capability** is a generalizable generative skill (e.g., T2I synthesis, image editing, image-knowledge association), while a **task** is a specific instance that exercises one or more capabilities (e.g., \"generate an image of a pangolin climbing a tree\" or \"replace the background in this image with a beach scene\"). Capabilities are the building blocks for tasks, and tasks are used to evaluate and train capabilities.\n\n## Capability Dependency Graph Construction\n\n### Identification Process\n\nDependencies among capabilities are identified through a hybrid process:\n\n1. **Manual Task Analysis:**  \n   We analyze the input-output requirements and prerequisite knowledge for each capability. For example, image editing requires the ability to generate images from text, as edits must be rendered realistically.\n\n2. **Empirical Ablation Studies:**  \n   We conduct controlled experiments where models are trained with and without specific capabilities. For instance, we train a model with only T2I data and test its performance on editing tasks, and vice versa. Criteria for ablation include:\n   - Downstream performance drop (e.g., >10% decrease in editing accuracy when T2I is withheld).\n   - Failure to generalize to composite tasks.\n   - Human rater agreement on qualitative degradation.\n\n3. **Domain Expert Consultation:**  \n   We select domain experts based on publication record and experience in generative modeling. Disagreements among experts are resolved through structured discussions and, if necessary, majority voting. For ambiguous cases, we run additional ablation studies to empirically validate the ordering.\n\n### Encoding and Example\n\nThe resulting dependency structure is encoded as a directed acyclic graph (DAG), where nodes represent capabilities and edges indicate prerequisite relationships. For example, T2I is a prerequisite for image editing, which in turn is a prerequisite for knowledge association. The curriculum follows a topological ordering of this graph, ensuring foundational skills are mastered before introducing dependent tasks.\n\n**Textual Description of Figure 1:**  \nImagine a diagram with three nodes:  \n- \"Text-to-Image (T2I)\" at the base,  \n- \"Image Editing\" above it, with an arrow from T2I to Image Editing,  \n- \"Image-Knowledge Association\" at the top, with an arrow from Image Editing to Image-Knowledge Association.  \nThis illustrates the prerequisite relationships among capabilities.\n\n## Data Engines and Interoperability\n\nOur infrastructure is built around three specialized yet interoperable data engines, each responsible for a specific type of supervision:\n\n1. **Text-Image Grounding Engine:**  \n   Collects and curates high-quality image-caption pairs for T2I generation, ensuring diverse and accurate textual descriptions.\n\n2. **Inter-Image Transformation Engine:**  \n   Constructs paired examples for image editing tasks, such as object addition, removal, or style transfer, by generating or mining before-and-after image pairs with corresponding transformation descriptions.\n\n3. **Image-Knowledge Association Engine:**  \n   Links images to structured knowledge entities, enabling models to connect visual content with external information such as object categories or factual attributes.\n\n### Interoperability and Data Flow\n\nThe three engines are interoperable in the following ways:\n- **Shared Supervision:** Outputs from the Text-Image Grounding Engine (e.g., high-quality captions) are used as inputs for the Inter-Image Transformation Engine to describe both the original and edited images.\n- **Cross-Engine Annotation:** The Image-Knowledge Association Engine leverages captions and transformation descriptions from the other engines to provide richer context for linking images to knowledge entities.\n- **Unified Data Schema:** All engines output data in a standardized format (image, text, optional transformation, optional knowledge entity), enabling seamless integration during training.\n- **Feedback Loop:** Performance gaps identified in one engine (e.g., poor editing of rare objects) can trigger targeted data generation or annotation in another engine (e.g., sourcing more T2I examples of the rare object).\n\n**Textual Description of Figure 2:**  \nEnvision a workflow diagram with three boxes (one for each engine), arrows showing data flowing from the Text-Image Grounding Engine to the Inter-Image Transformation Engine, and both feeding into the Image-Knowledge Association Engine. All engines output to a central training data pool.\n\n## Caption Experts and Expert Construction\n\n### Caption Experts\n\nCaption experts are responsible for generating, refining, and validating textual supervision. They consist of advanced language models for automated captioning and trained human annotators for review and correction. For foundational tasks, automated captioning models generate initial descriptions, which are then reviewed and refined by human annotators for accuracy and consistency. For more complex tasks, such as describing editing transformations or knowledge associations, human experts play a larger role in crafting or validating captions to ensure semantic correctness and interoperability across datasets.\n\n### Expert Construction\n\nExpert construction is a targeted process, distinct from caption experts, where either human annotators or specialized models create or refine supervision for challenging or underrepresented examples identified during evaluation. For instance, if the model underperforms on images involving rare objects, experts curate or generate new annotated examples to address these gaps. This process is triggered by gap-aware evaluation and is essential for maintaining data quality and coverage. To mitigate scalability issues, we prioritize automation for common cases and reserve human expert construction for high-impact or rare scenarios.\n\n## Curriculum Learning Protocol\n\nA multi-stage curriculum guides the training process, evolving task composition, visual-concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution according to the dependency order of capability acquisition.\n\n### Defining Curriculum Stages\n\n- **Stage 1:** Focuses on foundational T2I tasks using the text-image grounding engine.\n- **Stage 2:** Introduces image editing tasks, leveraging both T2I and inter-image transformation data.\n- **Stage 3:** Adds knowledge association tasks, integrating data from all three engines.\n\n### Transition Triggers and Dynamic Adjustment\n\nTransitions between stages are triggered by explicit performance thresholds on validation sets specific to each capability:\n\n- **For T2I (Stage 1):**  \n  FID \u2264 8.0 and CLIP score \u2265 0.29 on the CPI-Bench validation set. These thresholds are set based on the following rationale:  \n  - FID \u2264 8.0 corresponds to the point where generated images are visually indistinguishable from real images for most human raters, as established in [1,2].  \n  - CLIP score \u2265 0.29 matches the semantic alignment achieved by state-of-the-art models on the same benchmark, as reported in [2].  \n  - In our pilot experiments, models below these thresholds failed to support downstream editing tasks reliably.\n\n- **For image editing (Stage 2):**  \n  Editing accuracy \u2265 75% on a held-out set of transformation tasks. This threshold is chosen because human raters agree edits are reliably reflected in outputs at this level, and prior benchmarks [4] report diminishing returns above 75%."}}